<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 6<br>Part 1 (§§ 1–10 / Oaseyos א–י)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:24</span> (<i>Amtza'isa D'Alma</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — Performing the Mitzvah with Joy: The Nine Chambers</div>

<div class="p">Based on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Amtza'isa d'alma</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:24</span>). He explains there the supreme virtue of performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy — through which one merits raising the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> from her exile, etc., ascending ever higher until one merits grasping the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span> [reaching and not reaching]. This light is above <span class="h">nefesh, ruach, neshamah</span>, etc.; see all this there very well. Especially what he explains regarding the nine chambers that are not illuminated, etc. — which are fashioned through the <span class="h">tikunim</span> and ascents merited through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy. One ascends ever higher until the Arranger and Settler of the mental faculties is blessed — the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, etc. Then through the pursuit and the obstructer, the mental faculties are struck by the Settler and Arranger, and the nine chambers that are not illuminated are fashioned, etc. — <span class="mx">"none can stand within them, they do not cling and are not known"</span> etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">You, the student, must know and believe that everything stated there is accomplished through every single Jew when he merits strengthening himself to perform the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy. For <span class="v">"it is not in heaven"</span> — the Torah was not given to those who ascend to heaven, etc. — for <span class="v">"the matter is very close to us"</span> etc. This is not the place to elaborate on this.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — The Obligation of Thanksgiving: Joy Rectifies the Exile of the <i>Shechinah</i></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the obligation of thanksgiving upon all who emerge from distress — the four who must give thanks: wilderness-travelers, etc., those released from prison, the sick who are healed, and sea-travelers when they come up from there.</div>

<div class="p">All troubles, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, are the aspect of the exile of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> — for <span class="v">"in all their affliction, He is afflicted,"</span> as is known. The essential exile of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> is the overpowering of sadness upon the hearts of Israel, in the aspect of <span class="v">"He was grieved in His heart,"</span> as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. From there come all the desires — particularly the desire for relations and the desire for money — which come only from sadness. From there all troubles come, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, when Hashem helps and one emerges from the distress, one must bring a <span class="h">korban Todah</span> [thanksgiving-offering]. Now, when we have no <span class="h">korban</span>, one must give thanks verbally with all his heart — which is the aspect of joy. For the essential joy is thanksgiving to Hashem — the essential ultimate joy of the future, when we will engage only in <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving to Hashem with immense joy: <span class="v">"give thanks to Hashem with the harp; with the ten-stringed lyre sing to Him"</span> — the aspect of the ten types of melody. As it says: <span class="v">"a psalm for thanksgiving: call out to Hashem, all the earth; serve Hashem with joy"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">This is the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving one is obligated to give when emerging from distress — to rectify the damage from which the troubles came, which is the sadness, as above. So that henceforth we merit being saved from all troubles through the intensity of joy — until we merit all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, grasping the above-mentioned light, the essential purpose, as explained there. Therefore <span class="mx">"all the korbanos will be nullified except the korban Todah"</span> (Midrash Rabbah, Vayikra, Parshah 27).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — The Four Types of Bread: Ten Loaves = Ten Types of Melody</div>

<div class="p">For this reason one brings in the <span class="h">korban Todah</span> four types of bread, each consisting of ten loaves. The essential sadness clings to the bread, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"; "by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread."</span> Conversely, the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of eating is through joy: <span class="v">"eat your bread in joy,"</span> in the aspect of <span class="v">"eating and drinking and rejoicing"</span> etc. Therefore there are ten loaves of each type — corresponding to the ten types of melody, which are the source and totality of all joys.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — The Four Types of Bread in Detail: The Tikunim of the Todah</div>

<div class="p">One can also allude, in specific detail, to the four types of bread brought in the <span class="h">korban Todah</span>: they correspond to the <span class="h">tikunim</span> fashioned through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">korban Todah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">As explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, <span class="h">Malchus</span> rises from among the <span class="h">klipos</span>. When a person performs a <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> has the power to go and arouse all the worlds to serve Hashem, etc. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus</span> clothing <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, the instruments of walking, etc. Through the walking, blessing is drawn to all the worlds. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span> clothing <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span> — the hands, from which all blessings come, etc. The essential blessing flowing from the hands is intellect, etc. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span> clothing <span class="h">Chochmah-Binah-Da'as</span>, etc. Through this the Arranger is set in order, etc. — the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the four types of bread brought in the <span class="h">korban Todah</span> (= the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, as above) — corresponding to the above four aspects of <span class="h">tikunim</span> fashioned through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>:</div>

<div class="p"><b><span class="h">Chalos Matzos</span></b> [loaves of matzah] — the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus</span>, called <span class="h">chalah</span>, whose measure is 43 eggs, the aspect of <span class="mx">"gam zos"</span> [also this], as brought in Rabbainu's words <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Abba Shaul</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:55</span>). This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and even also this — when they are in the land of their enemies"</span> [i.e., even in exile]. The essential exile is sadness, as above. When one performs a <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy, he raises her from her exile, as above — the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus</span> rising from the <span class="h">klipos</span>, as above. This is accomplished through the <span class="h">chalos matzos</span> brought in the <span class="h">korban Todah</span>, which is the aspect of the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p"><b><span class="h">Rekikin</span></b> [wafers] — the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, the legs, subduing the <span class="h">klipos</span> whose hold is in the legs: <span class="v">"the iniquity of my heels surrounds me"</span> etc. — who want to overpower, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>: <span class="v">"it devoured and crushed, and the remainder it trampled with its feet"</span> (Daniel 7). But the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who perform <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy and merit Israel to do likewise — they will subdue and crush them: <span class="v">"it shall crush and destroy all these... and it shall stand forever"</span> (ibid. 2). The <span class="h">rekikin</span> [wafers] allude to this crushing [<span class="h">hadakah</span>]: <span class="v">"like mud in the streets I shall crush them"</span> etc., which is associated with <span class="h">rekikin</span>, as brought. Therefore it says of them <span class="v">"anointed with oil"</span> — alluding to <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who will crush the <span class="h">klipos</span> that seek to crush everything, whose hold is in the legs, as above. The essential battle of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> is in the aspect of the legs, as is known: <span class="v">"his feet shall stand on that day"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p"><b><span class="h">Murkeves b'roschin</span></b> [scalded in boiling water] — the aspect of the blessing of intellect, which is like a fire confined in the heart (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Abba Shaul</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:55</span>). <span class="h">Murkeves</span> has the same letters as <span class="h">m'varkes</span> [blessing] — the aspect of the blessing of the hands, which is the blessing of intellect.</div>

<div class="p">All the above three come as <span class="h">matzah</span>. Additionally, one must bring in the <span class="h">Todah</span> ten loaves of <span class="h">chametz</span>, as it says: <span class="v">"upon loaves of leavened bread."</span> This is the awesome wonder of the <span class="h">korban Todah</span> — it differs from all <span class="h">korbanos</span> in which <span class="h">chametz</span> is forbidden: <span class="v">"for any leaven... you shall not burn"</span> etc. Only in the <span class="h">korban Todah</span> is <span class="h">chametz</span> brought. For <span class="h">chametz</span>, at its holy root, derives from the aspect of the power of the Arranger and Settler of the mental faculties — the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, the aspect of waiting. When one asks a person some intellectual question, he says: "Wait until I settle my mind," etc. See there well in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> — the entire essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger and Settler, through which one merits grasping the above nine chambers through the pursuit and the obstructer, etc.; see there very well.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">chametz</span> at its holy root. <span class="h">Chametz</span> comes through waiting. (As explained at length in Hilchos Nefilas Apayim, Halachah 4, based on the above <span class="h">Torah</span>; see there well.) The mind and <span class="h">da'as</span> are drawn according to the eating that sustains a person — the essential vitality is wisdom and intellect: <span class="v">"wisdom gives life,"</span> as explained in our writings many times.</div>

<div class="p">When one has not rectified the Arranger and Settler (= <span class="h">Keser</span>), <span class="h">chametz</span> is forbidden with an exceedingly severe prohibition — the aspect of the prohibition of <span class="h">chametz</span> on <span class="h">Pesach</span>. On <span class="h">Pesach</span> we left the exile of Egypt, and it is explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> that the essential exile is the exile of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> — the overpowering of sadness upon the heart of the Jewish person. When one overpowers with joy, this is the essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> from exile: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out"</span> etc. Then one merits all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, until even the Arranger and Settler is rectified, etc. — through which one grasps the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>, through the above nine chambers.</div>

<div class="p">But it is impossible to merit all this except through the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span> [who will say?], explained there — entering the Chambers of Exchange, the place of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, and raising the holiness from there in the aspect of <span class="h">ketores</span>, as explained there. That is: through overpowering with joy, one raises the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> (= <span class="h">Malchus</span>) from her exile among the <span class="h">klipos</span>, meriting all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">But in Egypt, where the <span class="h">klipos</span> had a very strong hold, it was exceedingly difficult to merit this. The <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> was only through His abundant mercy and kindness — illuminating upon them with an immense illumination of the mental faculties, not in gradual stages, through an <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'aila</span> [arousal from Above]. For they did not have a proper <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> [arousal from below] to be worthy of <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>, as explained in the writings of the Arizal and brought in our writings many times. Therefore the Arranger and Settler (= <span class="h">Keser</span>) was not rectified then. The Arranger and Settler can only be rectified through achieving a complete <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — descending into the Chambers of Exchange and raising the holiness from there, etc. — the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>, as above. Therefore they had to leave in haste. For this reason, eating <span class="h">chametz</span> on <span class="h">Pesach</span> is forbidden with an exceedingly severe prohibition — it is forbidden to leaven the mind at all as long as the Arranger and Settler of the mental faculties has not been rectified, since the mind follows the eating, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Especially since at the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> from Egypt they needed to rectify the sin of Adam HaRishon — whose sin was in eating, eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil: forbidden eating. Through this one damages the <span class="h">da'as</span> greatly, entering knowledge and investigations that are forbidden to enter, transgressing the boundary of holiness — the aspect of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, etc. Since on <span class="h">Pesach</span> this needed rectification, the essential holiness of <span class="h">Pesach</span> pertains to eating: one must eat <span class="h">matzah</span>, and <span class="h">chametz</span> is exceedingly severely forbidden — for the Arranger and Settler has not yet been rectified, and it is forbidden to leaven the mind at all. Then <span class="h">chametz</span> is in the aspect of the damage of eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil — through which was decreed: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span> — sadness and melancholy clinging to eating, the essential exile, as above. Therefore one must eat only <span class="h">matzah</span>, the bread of the mighty, the aspect of <span class="h">man</span> [manna], the food of angels who have no <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — for then the food is drawn from Above through the immense illumination of mental faculties drawn in the aspect of <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'aila</span>. But we are forbidden to leaven our minds at all with these lofty mental faculties that descend temporarily for the sake of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>, as is known.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore <span class="h">chametz</span> is forbidden until after the splitting of the <span class="h">Yam Suf</span>, when the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span> began through the <span class="h">Sefirah</span> [counting of the Omer], etc. Afterward, on <span class="h">Shavuos</span> — when the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span> is accomplished, as is known — then we bring the Two Loaves specifically from <span class="h">chametz</span>. (As explained at length in Hilchos Nefilas Apayim; see there.)</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the <span class="h">Todah</span>, brought when one emerges from distress (= exile, whose essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through joy, as above), through which one merits the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger and Settler, etc. — therefore one brings <span class="h">chametz</span> bread. When the Arranger and Settler is rectified through the overpowering of joy, <span class="h">chametz</span> is rectified at its holy root — the aspect of waiting, etc., as above. Then through <span class="h">chametz</span> bread specifically, a wondrous <span class="h">tikun</span> is accomplished — the aspect of the Two Loaves of <span class="h">Shavuos</span> that come specifically from <span class="h">chametz</span>, because then one merits <span class="h">Keser</span>, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger, etc. As explained at length in Hilchos Nefilas Apayim; see there.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — The Four Who Give Thanks Correspond to the Four Types of Bread</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the four who must give thanks: wilderness-travelers, etc. These four correspond to the above four aspects, which in turn correspond to the four types of bread of the <span class="h">Todah</span>, as above. Each of the four who must give thanks encompasses all of them — therefore each must bring all four types of bread in the <span class="h">Todah</span>, and each needs all the <span class="h">tikunim</span> explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Nevertheless, each of the four who must give thanks corresponds specifically to one of the four above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, which corresponds to one type of bread from the four types. (I also saw in the Alshich, according to his holy approach, that he explains in <span class="h">Parshas Todah</span> that the four types of bread correspond to the four who must give thanks, even though each one is obligated to bring all four types; see there. This is a general principle everywhere: every aspect encompasses all the aspects, yet each has its own distinct aspect. Even in revealed matters this is so — for example, the four elements, where each is a distinct force: earth is cold and dry, fire is hot and dry, etc., yet each encompasses all. How much more so in all the wisdoms of <span class="h">Kabbalah</span>, as explained in the writings of the Arizal and brought in our writings.)</div>

<div class="p">The four who must give thanks are explained in Mizmor "<span class="h">Hodu</span>," as stated in the Gemara — from which we learn these four must give thanks. They are wilderness-travelers: <span class="v">"they wandered in the wilderness"</span> etc. This corresponds to <span class="h">Malchus</span> emerging from exile through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving, as above. Initially, when a person is in distress, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — and the essential distress and exile is sadness, as above — this is the aspect of wilderness-travelers: <span class="v">"they wandered in the wilderness on a desolate path"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">These four who must give thanks allude to every person specifically in this exile — what passes over every person at every time in the aspect of all the above four, as explained elsewhere. Hashem saves him at every moment. A person is obligated to thank Hashem every day for all the miracles and wonders He does for him every day — the many things one can see and understand, how Hashem saves him every day, for one who pays attention to his ways. And vastly more that he does not know at all — for <span class="mx">"the one who experienced the miracle does not recognize his miracle,"</span> as our Sages say. For this our Sages established many blessings and thanksgivings every day, especially in <span class="h">Birchas Modim</span>, recited three times daily: <span class="v">"we give thanks to You"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"for Your miracles that are with us every day, and for Your wonders and kindnesses at every time — evening, morning, and afternoon"</span> etc. And likewise in <span class="h">Nishmas</span> recited on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, and much more.</div>

<div class="p">Only <span class="h">Birchas HaGomel</span> with Hashem's Name and <span class="h">Malchus</span> is recited only when one of the above four actually passes over a person physically. But vastly many miracles and wonders pass over every person, akin to the above four — for which one is obligated to give much thanks every day with joy. The essential thing is to overpower with joy — to look upon the kindnesses and wonders of Hashem that He does for him every day, within the distress itself, to trust in His kindness and rejoice in His salvation. (As explained at length in our writings on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">the days of Chanukah are days of thanksgiving</span>," <span class="lr">LM II:2</span>, in Hilchos Kilai Behaimah and Hilchos Perikah U'Te'inah; see there.)</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of wilderness-travelers. When one is in the aspect of wilderness-travelers and emerges from there, he must give thanks with joy — thereby raising <span class="h">Malchus</span> from among the <span class="h">klipos</span>, the first of the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>. Initially he was in the aspect of one walking in the wilderness itself: <span class="v">"they wandered in the wilderness on a desolate path; they found no inhabited city; hungry and thirsty, their soul fainted within them."</span> Through crying out to Hashem, He saved him, and he must give thanks with joy. This is <span class="h">Malchus</span> rising from the <span class="h">klipos</span> — the aspect of the desolate wilderness and barren path. Through thanksgiving with joy, he merits leaving them entirely — the above-mentioned ascent of <span class="h">Malchus</span> from among the <span class="h">klipos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">There is also the aspect of one released from prison — corresponding to the second <span class="h">tikun</span>: the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the legs, by the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> going to arouse everything to Hashem, etc., as explained there. Correspondingly, the opposition seizes the aspect of the legs, the aspect of prison — the damage of the legs, not allowing him to go forth: <span class="v">"He has fenced me in and I cannot go out"</span>; <span class="v">"prisoners of affliction and iron"</span>; <span class="v">"He placed my feet in the stocks."</span> This is the aspect where even after one has left the desolate wilderness and is in settled territory — he has drawn close to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people of the generation who guide him on a straight path: <span class="v">"He guided them on a straight path to go to an inhabited city"</span> — yet when he wants to lift his legs to walk in Hashem's ways (= the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, the instruments of walking), the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> opposes the holy legs, seeking to imprison him as if in an actual jail, not allowing him to go to <span class="h">shuls</span> and <span class="h">batai midrash</span> to pray and study — all the more to walk spiritually in Hashem's ways and arouse others to His service. This is: <span class="v">"those who sit in darkness and the shadow of death, prisoners of affliction and iron"</span> — the aspect of those imprisoned. When he is saved from there, he must give thanks with joy — through which the above <span class="h">tikun</span> of the legs is drawn (= <span class="h">Malchus</span> clothing <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, as explained there).</div>

<div class="p">There is also the aspect of the sick person who is healed — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the hands (= <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span>). The essential power is in the hands, where the pulse is — upon which all types of illness and healing depend. Through the hands, the blessing of intellect is drawn, as explained there — from which comes all vitality: <span class="v">"wisdom gives life."</span> When one damages this, illnesses come, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — the corruption of vitality, the damage of the hands. When healed, one must give thanks with joy — the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the hands, through which the blessing of intellect is drawn, as above.</div>

<div class="p"><b>Sea-travelers in ships</b> — the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the mental faculties in completeness, through the Arranger and Settler being rectified, etc. — the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the mind. As long as the Arranger and Settler has not been rectified, it is impossible to achieve any perception due to the overabundance of light — one is in the aspect of sea-travelers upon whom the stormy wind overpowers, etc.</div>

<div class="p">Those <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people who merit, through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy, all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, until their mental faculties pursue to grasp the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, etc. — they are in the aspect of those who go down to the sea of wisdom in ships, doing work in great waters (= the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span>). Only they see and know how great are Hashem's works and wonders: <span class="v">"they"</span> specifically <span class="v">"saw the works of Hashem and His wonders in the deep."</span></div>

<div class="p">But sometimes the stormy wind overpowers, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>: <span class="v">"He spoke and the stormy wind arose, raising its waves; they ascend to the heavens, they descend to the depths"</span> etc. This comes from the Arranger and Settler of the mental faculties not being properly rectified — the screen that obstructs the mental faculties during their pursuit to grasp. Then it is impossible to reach their desired haven, for the mind becomes confused like a drunkard due to the overabundance of light. They are then in the aspect of <span class="v">"they ascend to heavens, they descend to depths"</span> — the ascent is not in proper order, and through this one is cast down to the depths. As Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> explains elsewhere on this verse (<span class="lr">LM I:163</span>).</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">tikun</span> of the mental faculties in completeness — essentially through the Arranger and Settler — is possible only through the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>, descending to the place of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, to the Chambers of Exchange, to sort the holiness from there, etc. Therefore, whoever wishes to approach the holy and merit all the above must be in the aspect of entering and exiting. The descent must precede the ascent — this is the aspect of what passes over every person, small and great. Even the most lowly in level, even the small and the young — everything that passes over them, that their mind is small in the aspect of <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>, and all desires and youthful follies overpower them — all derives from this aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>: the Chambers of Exchange, the <span class="h">klipah</span> that precedes the fruit, which passes over every person in the world. From there come all the descents that precede the ascents of every person. The more one strives to achieve some ascent in holiness, the more the Chambers of Exchange overpower with desires, fantasies, confusions, and reversed reasonings, etc. One needs great exertion and closeness to <span class="h">tzadikim</span> until he merits sorting the holiness from there and ascending in peace. The essential thing is through performing <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with great joy, as above — through which one raises the holiness from the Chambers of Exchange, ascending through all the above ascents until the Arranger and Settler is rectified (the essential thing). Then through the pursuit of the mental faculties and the obstructer, one merits the above nine chambers, until one grasps, etc.</div>

<div class="p">But when one has not properly sorted the holiness from the Chambers of Exchange — and therefore has not rectified the Arranger and Settler — one is in the aspect of <span class="v">"they ascend to heavens, they descend to depths"</span>; <span class="v">"they reel and stagger like a drunkard, and all their wisdom is swallowed up."</span> All his wisdom is swallowed and confused like a drunkard — because he lacks the power of the Arranger and Settler, through which the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the mental faculties' perception is accomplished, as above. When he merits being saved from this, he must give thanks with joy — the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger and Settler, as above: the aspect of the <span class="h">chametz</span> bread of the <span class="h">Todah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"He stills the storm to calm, and their waves are hushed"</span> — stated regarding the <span class="h">tikun</span> of sea-travelers. Their <span class="h">tikun</span> is through the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger and Settler (= <span class="h">Keser</span>), where the secret of silence resides: <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, as explained elsewhere. The screen (= <span class="h">Keser</span>) that obstructs the mental faculties: <span class="mx">"wait until I settle my mind"</span> — this is the aspect of silence: one is hinted, "Be silent and do not be hasty to respond; rather, be silent in your thought until you settle" — through which the essential proper perception is accomplished. Fortunate is one who merits this. Therefore it concludes there: <span class="v">"they rejoiced when they were stilled, and He guided them to their desired haven."</span> The essential thing is through joy — through which one merits the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span>, the aspect of silence, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 6 — "His Kindness and His Wonders to the Children of Men"</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness and His wonders to the children of men."</span> <span class="v">"His kindness"</span> — the aspect of the illumination of the mental faculties through the blessing of the hands (= <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span>). The essential is <span class="h">Chesed</span>, for <span class="mx">"Kel is the light of wisdom, and it is the day that goes with all the days"</span>: <span class="v">"by day Hashem commands His kindness,"</span> as brought in the holy writings and in our writings. <span class="v">"His wonders"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, the Arranger and Settler. <span class="h">Keser</span> is called <span class="h">Peleh</span> [wonder], as is known.</div>

<div class="p">The essential thanksgiving is for Hashem helping one emerge from all the above troubles of the four — through the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, until one merits the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the mental faculties (= <span class="h">chesed</span>) and the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span> (= <span class="h">peleh</span> [wonder]). This is: <span class="v">"let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness"</span> — regarding <span class="h">chesed</span> it says <span class="v">"to Hashem"</span>; regarding <span class="v">"His wonders"</span> it says <span class="v">"to the children of men."</span> This is the aspect of the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the pursuit and the obstructer. The mental faculties pursue to grasp the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, wanting to ascend to Hashem. According to their pursuit, one would be absorbed there, nullified from existence, and the world would be nullified — people would not know His greatness and wonders, for which He created everything. Therefore the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the obstructer is needed — the aspect of <span class="h">Peleh</span>, <span class="h">Keser</span>, which obstructs the mental faculties from their pursuit. Through this, the above nine chambers are fashioned, through which one grasps the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>. From there come all the perceptions and knowledge of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — through which they know how to contract the light of their perception until they can make known to us His awesome wonders. This is: <span class="v">"let them give thanks to Hashem for His kindness and His wonders to the children of men"</span> — <span class="h">chesed</span> is the pursuit of the mind, pursuing toward Hashem to grasp His light. But this alone is not completeness — one would be nullified from existence and people would not know His greatness. Through the aspect of <span class="h">Peleh</span>, one can make known the wonders of His greatness to the children of men. This is: <span class="v">"His wonders to the children of men,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of all the verses urging us to tell His wonders and speak of them — <span class="v">"wonders"</span> specifically: <span class="v">"speak of all His wonders"</span>; <span class="v">"I shall speak of the words of Your wonders"</span>; <span class="v">"tell among the nations His glory, among all peoples His wonders"</span>; <span class="v">"I shall give thanks to Hashem with all my heart; I shall tell all Your wonders"</span> (Tehillim 9); <span class="v">"to hear the voice of thanksgiving and tell all Your wonders"</span> (ibid. 26); <span class="v">"until now I shall tell Your wonders"</span> (ibid. 71); and many more. The essential greatness and glory of Hashem is to make known to the children of men His might and awesome wonders — especially to those very far away, as explained (<span class="lr">LM I:10</span>): the essential greatness of Hashem is that even the idol-worshippers and those exceedingly far away know Him. Therefore He mentions <span class="v">"wonders"</span> every time — the essential ability to speak and tell His greatness to the children of men is through the aspect of the Supernal <span class="h">Peleh</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>, the Arranger and Settler that strikes the mental faculties and obstructs their pursuit. Through this the above nine chambers are fashioned — vessels to perceive what is perceived, through which specifically one can know and make known and tell His wonders to the children of men, as above — the essential glory and greatness of the Creator, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the obligation of thanksgiving is specifically in public, as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"one must give thanks before ten"</span>: <span class="v">"let them exalt Him in the congregation of the people"</span> etc. — for this is the essential greatness, as above. This is: <span class="v">"my feet stood on level ground; in assemblies I shall bless Hashem."</span> <span class="v">"My feet stood on level ground"</span> — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the legs, the beginning of the <span class="h">tikun</span> through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy, through which the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> goes to arouse everything to Hashem. Through this one merits all the above ascents until the Arranger is rectified, etc. — through which one can tell His wonders to the children of men, as above. Therefore adjacent to this: <span class="v">"in assemblies I shall bless Hashem"</span> — specifically through <span class="v">"my feet stood on level ground"</span> (= the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the legs through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy), through which the obstructer (= <span class="h">Keser</span>) is rectified, as above — through this, <span class="v">"in assemblies I shall bless Hashem"</span>: one can bless and thank Him in assemblies, in public, in congregations of myriads of people — which is the essential thing, as above. This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"show wonders with Your kindness"</span> — <span class="v">"wonders"</span> and <span class="v">"kindness"</span> specifically: the aspect of <span class="v">"His kindness and His wonders,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 7 — Before Ten, Two of Them Torah Scholars: The Pursuit and the Obstructer</div>

<div class="p">This is why one must give thanks before ten, two of them Torah scholars. Ten — corresponding to the ten types of melody, the source of all songs, praises, thanksgivings, and hymns, all encompassed in the Book of Tehillim composed by David HaMelech — <span class="v">"the sweet singer of Israel"</span> — who encompasses the ten types of melody, as is known. This is the aspect of the great virtue of a <span class="h">minyan</span> of ten from Israel — then specifically we recite <span class="h">Bor'chu</span> and <span class="h">Kedushah</span>, etc. Wherever there are ten from Israel, the ten types of melody are aroused.</div>

<div class="p">Two of them Torah scholars — who are masters of <span class="h">da'as</span> and mental faculties. Therefore they are two — corresponding to the pursuit and the obstructer. When there are two Torah scholars, there is certainly the aspect of pursuit and obstruction — the virtue of studying with a companion: one asks his fellow because his mind pursues to grasp and understand the matter properly, and his fellow responds: "Wait until I settle my mind" — as this matter is explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Likewise in the roots of their souls in the perception of G-dliness — through which the nine chambers are grasped, the essential thanksgiving, the aspect of joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 8 — Training Oneself Every Day in Joyful Thanksgiving</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must accustom himself very much, every day and at every moment, to give thanks and praise to Hashem for all the good He has bestowed upon us. Even in the intensity of the soul's distress, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span> — even when one has descended and become distant however he has become distant, however he is — let him turn everything to gladness and joy: <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain"</span> etc., as explained elsewhere. For despite everything, one still merits reciting <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> and unifying His Name twice daily, believing in Hashem and in Moshe His servant — the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation — etc. <span class="v">"How many good stages has the Omnipresent bestowed upon us"</span> etc. <span class="v">"Were our mouths filled with song"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">For the essential exile of the soul is the sadness, as understood from this <span class="h">Torah</span>; see there. The more sadness overpowers, the more desires overpower — especially the madness of the all-encompassing desire — and it cycles back, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, compounding with the desire for money and the distraction of livelihood: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span> etc. As explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">U'v'Yom HaBikkurim</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:56</span>) regarding <span class="v">"sadness of the hands"</span> etc. — until his soul nearly expires, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, were it not for Hashem helping us, illuminating upon us from afar through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, to gladden our souls through the deep counsel-paths of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Every person must force himself greatly with all his strength to draw joy upon himself. Therefore he must be very accustomed to joyful thanksgiving, as above — as explained at length in our writings on the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">the days of Chanukah are days of thanksgiving</span>" (Hilchos Kilai Behaimah 4 and Hilchos Perikah U'Te'inah 4; see there well).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — Mizmor Hodu on <i>Erev Shabbos</i>: The Exile of This World and <i>Shabbos</i> the Ultimate Purpose</div>

<div class="p">All the matters of the four who must give thanks pass over every person at every time — sometimes literally in the physical, but mostly through the various upheavals passing over a person every day, especially in the matter of the soul's exile, as explained above (in ois heh). Therefore it was established in these generations to recite Mizmor "<span class="h">Hodu</span>" every <span class="h">Erev Shabbos</span>, at the onset of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>. The six weekdays are the aspect of this world, called <span class="h">Erev Shabbos</span>: <span class="mx">"one who toiled on Erev Shabbos"</span> etc. <span class="h">Shabbos</span> is the aspect of the World to Come.</div>

<div class="p">It is known that this world is full of troubles and pains: <span class="v">"all his days are anger and pain"</span>; <span class="v">"man is born to toil."</span> As brought in the Midrash (Beraishis Rabbah 13): <span class="mx">"man was created only for toil — if he merited, he toils in Torah; fortunate is he"</span> etc. All our vitality and sustenance is only the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — the World to Come. This alone is our entire purpose. Man did not come to this world to endure such toil, sorrow, and various troubles impossible to describe — as each person knows in his soul. The wise who examine the world in general know the entire world is full of sorrows, anger, and pains, as stated in the above Midrash: man was created only for toil. Even the wise of the nations investigated this and speak of it extensively, as brought in Sefer Mivchar HaPeninim: man in this world is like one surrounded by wild beasts and enemy ambushes at every exit and entrance — if he escapes some, the rest will tear him apart, etc. (As explained in our writings elsewhere at length.)</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, certainly Hashem did not create man with such wondrous and awesome wisdom just to endure such toil and anger in this world. His intention is certainly only for good, as explained in all the <span class="h">sefarim</span>: He created the entire world and all it contains for the sake of man, to show His goodness, kindness, and compassion, as stated at the beginning of the Eitz Chaim (and in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Bo El Paroh</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:64</span>). The essential creation of man, for whose sake everything was created, was for the sake of the purpose — <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, the purpose of the creation of heaven and earth (as explained in <span class="lr">LM II:39</span>).</div>

<div class="p">This is all our consolation for all the troubles and sorrows each person endures: <span class="v">"this one shall comfort us from our deeds and from the sadness of our hands"</span> — said regarding Noach, who is the aspect of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> (as explained in <span class="lr">LM II:2</span>) — the aspect of the World to Come.</div>

<div class="p">As stated in the above Midrash: man was created only for toil — if he merited, he toils in Torah; if not, he toils in the earth. Fortunate is the one who toils in Torah. Our Sages also say in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 99): <span class="mx">"all bodies are porterage-bearers; fortunate is one who merited to be a porterage-bearer of Torah."</span></div>

<div class="p">But how does one merit this? The <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> and his forces overpower and spread so much over every person, not allowing him to pay attention to this. All from the profusion of troubles in this world — through which melancholy and sadness overpower, through which one cannot settle his mind as to what he is doing in this world, etc. (As explained in <span class="lr">LM II:10</span>.)</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must overpower very, very greatly to draw joy upon himself — by looking toward the end, the ultimate World to Come, the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, when everything will be turned to good. Even the most lowly in Israel, the worst of the worst — there is no measure to all the delightful wondrous good he will merit then. As the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> strengthened us in many expressions regarding this: <span class="v">"on that day the sin of Israel will be sought and it will be gone"</span> — as brought (<span class="lr">LM I:22</span>): they will seek and search for the sins of Israel, etc. — for they will be transformed into merits. As stated on the verse: <span class="v">"my spikenard gave its fragrance"</span>; and in many verses. Through this one can gladden himself even now, in everything passing over him: <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain, and sorrow and sighing shall flee"</span> — as explained (<span class="lr">LM II:23</span>): one must seize the sorrow and sighing and transform them to joy, etc.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 10 — "Who Will Say?": Drawing the Geulah into the Present Through Joy</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of what is explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: the elders of the academy said to him, <span class="h">"mi yaimar"</span> — who is the one who will enter the Chambers of Exchange, the place of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, and raise the holiness from there, etc. He answered them: <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu"</span> — the essential raising of holiness is through joy, and in the future the joy will be immense and through it we will leave the exile, etc.; see there.</div>

<div class="p">At first glance this is difficult: the gist of Rabbainu's words there is that he answered them that in the future, when we leave the exile, the joy will be very great, and through this the <span class="h">klipos</span> will be nullified, etc.; see there. If so, it is difficult — we still do not understand what we should do now, in the depth of this exile, where the essential exile is the sadness, etc. How will we merit now to gladden our souls, to subdue the <span class="h">klipos</span>, to leave the exile of the soul — the sadness that overpowers every day?</div>

<div class="p">It is known that the essential thing is to draw upon ourselves even now the aspect of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: <span class="v">"draw close to my soul, redeem it,"</span> as they say in the name of the Baal Shem Tov <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span>. All the words of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — especially the words of our master, teacher, and rabbi <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> — are all counsel to revive our souls now, even now, each and every one wherever he is.</div>

<div class="p">If so, we must contemplate his above words: what shall we do now? How does one leave the Chambers of Exchange — through joy. How shall we merit joy now, in this bitter exile where sadness overpowers both generally and individually, upon every Jewish soul?</div>

<div class="p">But it is according to the above. As Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> explains elsewhere (<span class="lr">LM I:250</span>): when the Holy One, blessed be He, wishes to save Israel, He draws <span class="h">hashgachah</span> from the ultimate end, etc.; see there. So too here: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananya answered them that at the ultimate end, when Israel leaves the exile, the joy will be very great — the aspect of <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu"</span> etc., as explained there. He hinted with this that through this very thing, each and every Jewish soul is extracted from its exile even now. The essential exile is sadness, as above. The counsel is: let one remind himself of the ultimate end — that in the end of all ends, Hashem will certainly save Israel, both generally and individually, as above. Through this one can gladden himself even now. Through this one leaves his exile, from the Chambers of Exchange, and sorts the holiness from there — until one merits grasping the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, etc., as above.</div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Part 1 (§§ 1–10 / Oaseyos א–י) · Continues in Part 2 ✦<br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

